---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SectionTitle from "../components/SectionTitle.astro";
import OresCode from "../components/OresCode.astro";
import EditorThemePicker from "../components/EditorThemePicker.astro";
import MemoryProfiles from "../components/MemoryProfiles.astro";
import { languageProgress } from "../data/language-progress.mjs";

import { languageMotionExamples as motion } from "../data/language-motion-examples.mjs";
const { pipelineCode, mlCode, httpCode, selectCode } = motion;

const structCode = `
const account = infer struct {
  id: "acct_42",
  active: true
};

match account over
  on structural {id: string, active: bool} a -> {
    return a.id;
  }
  on _ -> {
    return "unknown";
  }
end
`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="AttoLang — isolation-first systems programming"
  description="AttoLang is a stateful-first language and actor runtime inspired by Erlang, Rust, and Microsoft Orleans—built for WebSockets, games, distributed coordination, messaging, and safe native deployment."
  home={true}
>
  <section class="hero">
    <div>
      <p class="kicker">Isolation-first language design</p>
      <h1><span class="display-word"><img class="brand-logo" src="/attolang-wordmark.svg" alt="AttoLang" width="1100" height="283" /></span><br />safe code moves fast.</h1>
      <p class="hero-copy">
        AttoLang was motivated by the need to create more stateful applications, as opposed to
        stateless REST APIs: WebSocket servers, gaming backends, UIs, state machines for distributed coordination, message queues, and development servers.
      </p>
      <p class="hero-platforms">Built for <strong>GraalVM</strong> and <strong>LLVM</strong></p>
      <p class="hero-tldr">
        <span class="hero-tldr-label">In short,</span>
        <code class="hero-equation"><span class="hero-equation-name">AttoLang</span><span class="hero-equation-operator"> = ~(</span><span class="hero-equation-language">erlang</span><span class="hero-equation-operator"> + </span><span class="hero-equation-language">rust</span><span class="hero-equation-operator">)</span></code>
      </p>
      <p><a href="/erlang-rust-oreslang/">How Erlang and Rust ideas come together in AttoLang — 24-feature matrix →</a></p>
    </div>
    <aside class="hero-note">
      <strong>Built for hostile boundaries.</strong>
      <p>Mobile sandboxes. FaaS runtimes. Multi-tenant actors. Trusted and untrusted code living in one system without sharing the wrong things.</p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="stateful-applications">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="00 / Motivation" title="A next-generation language for stateful apps" href="/about/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="manifesto-lede">
          AttoLang was motivated by the need to create more stateful applications, as opposed to
          stateless REST APIs. Think WebSocket servers, gaming backends, UIs, state machines for
          distributed coordination, message queues, and development servers.
        </p>
        <p>
          AttoLang takes the best from <strong>Erlang</strong>, <strong>Rust</strong>, and
          projects like <strong>Microsoft Orleans</strong>, bringing them together in a
          next-generation language and runtime.
        </p>
        <p>
          AttoLang's performance ambition is to exceed Erlang's throughput while using
          substantially fewer compute resources, while pursuing the type and memory safety
          goals associated with Rust. The often-quoted 10× speed and one-tenth compute figures
          are design targets, not measured results; no controlled, like-for-like Erlang
          comparison establishes them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Design goals include full ahead-of-time (AOT) compilation, native binaries,
          zero-GC execution, fault-tolerant actors, and better hot upgrades. These are
          backend-specific goals, not blanket shipping guarantees; the verification matrix
          records what is implemented and tested today.
        </p>
        <p><a href="/stateful-applications/">See how AttoLang brings persistent actors, messaging, fault tolerance, and hot upgrades together →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="about">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="01 / About" title="Why AttoLang" href="/about/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          AttoLang starts from a different default: code units should have explicit boundaries,
          actor messages should be safe to move, and the compiler should reject whole categories
          of lifetime and data-flow mistakes before the runtime needs to clean them up.
        </p>
        <div class="card-grid">
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Isolation first</h3>
            <p>Actors are not just syntax for queues. Shared, private, isolated, and untrusted actors carry different safety and visibility contracts.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Compile-time leverage</h3>
            <p>Borrowing, structural types, options, sendability rules, and AOT-friendly shapes make data movement visible to the compiler.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Code sharing without data leaks</h3>
            <p>Actors in one OresVM can share immutable code images while retaining isolated state and controlled capabilities.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="card">
            <h3>Runtime plurality</h3>
            <p>The model is meant to fit phones, server processes, FaaS workers, GPU paths, and other constrained execution environments.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <p>
          AttoLang is authored by <a href="https://github.com/oresoftware">Oresoftware Dev House</a>.
          Click the section title to open the dedicated About page.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-philosophy" id="philosophy">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="02 / Philosophy" title="Spend syntax to buy certainty" href="/philosophy/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="manifesto-lede">
          In the age of AI-assisted programming, terseness is less sacred. AttoLang is more explicit
          when those extra tokens reduce ambiguity and make static analysis, AOT compilation, and
          JIT specialization faster.
        </p>


        <section class="language-shifts-home" aria-labelledby="language-shifts-home-title">
          <p class="eyebrow">From 2010–2022, and beyond</p>
          <h3 id="language-shifts-home-title">Twelve shifts. One language ambition.</h3>
          <p>AttoLang draws on what programming learned—and asks how those strengths can work together.</p>
          <ul class="language-shifts-checklist">
            {languageProgress.map((theme) => (
              <li><span class="progress-check" aria-hidden="true">✓</span><span>{theme.label}</span></li>
            ))}
          </ul>
          <p class="progress-caveat">
            This checks off <em>ideas worth addressing</em>, not finished AttoLang features.
            <a href="/philosophy/#language-progress">Explore the shifts, AttoLang's response, and the work ahead →</a>
          </p>
        </section>

        <div class="terminal-trace terminal-trace-home code-editor" data-code-editor="true" data-code-key="home-runtime-trace" data-editor-theme="vscode" aria-label="Illustrative AttoLang runtime stack trace">
          <div class="terminal-bar">
            <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
            <span class="terminal-dot"></span>
            <strong>ores · runtime</strong>
            <EditorThemePicker label="home runtime trace" />
          </div>
          <pre><code><span class="term-prompt">$</span> ores run app.ores --mode=hybrid
<span class="term-dim">[oresvm]</span> loaded code image app.aot <span class="term-tag">AOT</span>
<span class="term-dim">[oresvm]</span> Worker.run crossed hot threshold <span class="term-tag">JIT tier 2</span>

<span class="term-error">OresError[E_OPTION_NONE]</span>: unwrap() called on Option.none
  --> src/worker.ores:18:14
  18 | const job = next.unwrap();
     |                  ^^^^^^

stack trace:
  0: Worker.run(Job)          src/worker.ores:18
  1: QueueActor.dispatch(Job) src/queue.ores:44
  2: actor#worker-17.mailbox  &lt;oresvm&gt;</code></pre>
        </div>

        <div class="compile-strip">
          <strong>AOT for startup. JIT for heat.</strong>
          <p>
            AttoLang's explicit static surface is designed to feed both ahead-of-time compilation for
            predictable startup and deployment, and runtime specialization of hot code paths when a JIT is available.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="memory-home-intro" id="memory-management">
          <p class="eyebrow">Memory, deliberately</p>
          <h3>Actor-owned arenas on both backends.</h3>
          <p>
            LLVM targets zero tracing GC in verified native paths. JVM/GraalVM
            targets minimal managed GC with actor-owned FFM arenas.
            Per-actor allocation ownership helps reduce heap pressure, but
            does not by itself create an independent collector per actor.
          </p>
        </div>
        <MemoryProfiles />
        <p class="memory-profile-status">
          <strong>Design target, not a shipping guarantee.</strong>
          LLVM no-GC and GraalVM actor-owned arena work are in draft compiler/runtime
          PRs. Separate actor-owned memory regions must not be confused with
          separately collected physical heaps.
          <a href="/how-it-works/#memory-management">Read the memory-management contract →</a>
        </p>

        <div class="keyword-rail" aria-label="AttoLang design themes">
          <span>borrow checker</span>
          <span>no nulls</span>
          <span>static types</span>
          <span>exhaustive match</span>
          <span>backend-specific GC</span>
          <span>hot reload</span>
          <span>non-cooperative scheduling</span>
          <span>GPU / quantum</span>
        </div>

        <p>
          The safety stack is layered: Rust-class memory-safety goals through ownership and borrowing,
          option types instead of null, deterministic native lifetimes or GC for managed graphs, actor confinement for easier
          concurrency safety, and explicit effect/error boundaries such as <code>pure</code> and <code>trap</code>.
          <code>alex</code>/<code>nlex</code> lexical modes are designed to expose more optimization intent to the compiler.
        </p>

        <div class="future-strip">
          <strong>Future: location-transparent actors.</strong>
          <p>
            Keep one typed actor model while placement evolves from local shared memory to isolated memory,
            IPC, TCP, or an external machine. Placement affinity should change transport—not application semantics.
          </p>
        </div>

        <p><a href="/philosophy/">Read the full philosophy, runtime direction, and language-building notes →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-paradigms" id="paradigms">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="03 / Paradigms" title="More than one way to be fast" href="/paradigms/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="manifesto-lede">
          AttoLang is intentionally multi-paradigm: actor isolation for concurrency, functional
          composition for data flow, data-oriented layouts for throughput, hot-loading for change,
          and contracts for large static codebases.
        </p>

        <div class="paradigm-grid">
          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>ACTORS</span>
            <h3>Actor model + Graal isolates</h3>
            <p>Use actors as the primary concurrency boundary, with isolated execution available when mutable memory or third-party code must be confined.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>FUNCTIONAL</span>
            <h3>Functions as values</h3>
            <p>First-class functions, native map/filter/reduce, rx-ores style composition, and tail-call optimization for recursive functional workloads.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>DATA</span>
            <h3>Data-oriented programming</h3>
            <p>Shape runtime data for the hardware: cache-friendly CPU layouts, fewer indirections, SIMD-friendly iteration, GPU-friendly buffers, and deliberate AoS/SoA choices. Compiler and JIT visibility are benefits—not the definition.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>HOT LOAD</span>
            <h3>Development and production</h3>
            <p>Reload code during development, or introduce a new isolated actor generation in production and move traffic without mutating the old actor in place.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>COMPILE</span>
            <h3>AOT, JIT, or hybrid</h3>
            <p>AOT for predictable startup, JIT for hot-path specialization, or a hybrid model that starts from compiled code and optimizes what becomes hot.</p>
          </article>

          <article class="paradigm-card">
            <span>CONTRACTS</span>
            <h3>Static architecture</h3>
            <p>Module contracts and static-member contracts for classes let APIs and static surfaces be checked without pretending modules and classes are the same thing.</p>
          </article>
        </div>

        <p><a href="/paradigms/">Explore AttoLang's programming and runtime paradigms →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="examples">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="04 / Examples" title="The language in motion" href="/examples/" />
      <div class="code-stack">
        <OresCode caption="Three actors, one typed pipeline" code={pipelineCode} />
        <OresCode caption="Actor-based AI/ML training with backpropagation (design sketch)" code={mlCode} />
        <OresCode caption="Unary HTTP actor: Fetch-style response (proposed)" code={httpCode} />
        <OresCode caption="Select one ready channel operation" code={selectCode} />
        <OresCode caption="Closed inferred structs + structural matching" code={structCode} />
        <p class="progress-caveat">Illustrative AttoLang design syntax, not compiler-verified examples. Empty actors show topology; ML and HTTP APIs are proposed. <a href="/language/">See current language verification status →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="problem-spaces">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="05 / Application areas" title="Application Areas" href="/problem-spaces/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          AttoLang is aimed at domains where ordinary process boundaries are too expensive,
          shared memory is too risky, dense compute needs hardware-aware data layouts, or a runtime
          must safely host code it does not fully trust.
        </p>
        <div class="problem-list">
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>HPC + data-oriented systems</h3>
            <p>Actors orchestrate state and I/O while dense kernels target cache locality, SIMD, columnar/SoA layouts, and CPU/GPU execution.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Data science beyond Python</h3>
            <p>Target Python-class ergonomics with static types, ownership, actors, numerical/columnar data, AOT/JIT execution, and a path to GPU acceleration.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Erlang / Elixir + Phoenix successor</h3>
            <p>Keep the actor, supervision, messaging, and hot-code strengths while adding stronger static safety, isolation, and data-oriented performance.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>FaaS runtimes + workerd alternative</h3>
            <p>Build multi-tenant runtimes from shared, isolated, and untrusted actors with capabilities, quotas, hot generations, and non-cooperative scheduling.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Isolation on phones and FaaS</h3>
            <p>Use actors and capabilities to create cheap boundaries inside one process, with stronger isolation for workloads that need it.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>True data safety</h3>
            <p>Option-aware APIs, compiler-enforced ownership/borrowing, immutable values, and serialized actor mailboxes reduce ambiguous lifetimes and pointer leaks.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Hot-loading trusted and untrusted code</h3>
            <p>Load code without duplicating immutable code images across every actor, while whitelisting what untrusted actors are allowed to execute or share.</p>
          </article>
          <article class="problem">
            <h3>Cooperative scheduling + scheduling un-cooperative code</h3>
            <p>FaaS and mobile runtimes need a path for CPU-hungry or third-party work that cannot rely on cooperative scheduling alone.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="links">
    <div class="section-grid">
      <SectionTitle eyebrow="06 / Links" title="Follow the source" href="/links/" />
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The project is being designed and implemented in the open.</p>
        <div class="link-list">
          <a href="https://github.com/ores-truffle-oreslang"><span>AttoLang GitHub organization</span><span>↗</span></a>
          <a href="https://github.com/ores-truffle-oreslang/oreslang-source.java"><span>Compiler / VM source</span><span>↗</span></a>
          <a href="https://github.com/oresoftware"><span>Oresoftware Dev House</span><span>↗</span></a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
